What this file is, and what it is not

Renderizr is MIT-licensed, not Apache-2.0, so this file is not an Apache-2.0
section 4(d) NOTICE and imposes no section 4(d) obligation on anyone who
redistributes Renderizr. It exists for a more practical reason: Renderizr's
output is a bundle of minified third-party code with every licence header
stripped by the minifier, and this is the attribution block that has to travel
alongside that output for the MIT, BSD, Apache-2.0 and MPL-2.0 terms of the
bundled components to be satisfied.

It is written to be self-sufficient, because the whole premise of shipping it
is that it may be the only file a recipient gets. So it carries, below, the
copyright line and resolved version of every component that ships in rendered
output; the verbatim permission notice MIT section 1 requires; the verbatim
conditions and disclaimer BSD-2-Clause and BSD-3-Clause require; a URL to the
Apache-2.0 licence text, which Apache-2.0 section 4(a) requires recipients be
given; and the source-availability statement MPL-2.0 section 3.2(b) requires.
Nothing here is a substitute for reading THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md, which has the
provenance and the full licence texts — but this file on its own discharges the
obligations it names.

Neither of the Apache-2.0 works Renderizr uses — the Structurizr project and
the TypeScript compiler — ships a NOTICE file of its own, so there is nothing
to propagate under section 4(d). Verified on 2026-08-16 against structurizr
commit 9ff16634c3b8574584262ae8545510bbb1d1b4bd (no NOTICE anywhere in the
repository) and node_modules/typescript (ThirdPartyNoticeText.txt only, which
covers TypeScript's own dependencies and is not a section 4(d) NOTICE for
TypeScript).

THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md is the authoritative document: full licence texts,
exact versions, and which components ship in rendered output versus which are
build-time only. docs/licensing.md explains how to make this attribution reach
the pages Renderizr generates.

